sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: docs/cli/firewall/index.mdx; ADR 0244 and ADR 0245; Swamp shape.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-03, rule 22 (no script: one ufw argv per change over @swamp/ssh exec; a typed step plans positions).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: firewall:allow
  %% kind: firewall.allow
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: firewall:allow PORT<br/>--node=NODE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit firewall:allow PORT --node=NODE`
  %%> - syntax: `<port> --node=ID [--from=SOURCE] [--protocol=tcp|udp] [--label=TEXT]`
  Note over GW: Validate, record the rule
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target (`node_access.required`); the target is Linux (`firewall.platform_unsupported`)
  %%> - the port, source, protocol and label pass validation; admission refusals create no rule or Operation and change nothing on the Node
  %%> - no other firewall change holds the target (`operation.target_busy`); one lock per Node, including the protected-access skip
  alt repeats protected access
    %% path: protected-port-skip
    Note over GW: Skipped — already applied<br/>firewall.rule_exists
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the ports lie inside one protected port or range Orbit opens, and the source is one that entry admits
    %%> - a range extending beyond that entry or a source it does not admit takes the operator-rule path
    %%> Result:
    %%> - records a succeeded Operation without an operator rule, Swamp run or Node contact; this branch uses recorded intent, not a UFW check
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>succeeded Operation
  else operator rule
    %% path: operator-rule
    %%> Requirements:
    %%> - an opposite action for the same port, protocol and normalized source is refused (`firewall.rule_conflict`)
    %%> - a both-protocol rule beside a single-protocol rule on the same port and source is refused, for either action (`firewall.rule_overlap`)
    %%> Allowed:
    %%> - different source scopes may overlap; narrower sources take precedence regardless of action
    %%> - a repeat reuses the identity record and checks the Node in a new Operation; a label-only repeat updates the recorded label without rewriting correct UFW comments
    %%> Result:
    %%> - records the new or reused rule as provisioning and captures the full non-secret intent; nothing has happened on the Node yet
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-firewall-direct
    %%> Inputs:
    %%> - the exact rule tuple and comment, target address and pinned SSH host keys
    %%> - the shared SSH identity comes from the worker's swamp role; steps never call the Gateway
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation ID
    SW->>N: Read the rules<br/>ufw status · @swamp/ssh exec
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads numbered UFW rows to plan insert positions and detect drift on a repeat
    %%> - inactive UFW fails with `firewall.backend_inactive` before mutation; never enables the firewall
    Note over SW: Plan the changes
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - plans missing lines and moves misplaced lines; leaves identical, well-placed lines and their comments untouched
    %%> - only overlapping ports, protocols and source networks can make an existing line misplaced; unrelated managed trust does not force a move
    %%> - managed allows, including interface- and destination-scoped trust, do not force an existing allow to move: they cannot change its verdict; an overlapping deny still requires a move
    %%> - places new operator lines above Orbit's broad managed rules and narrower sources above broader ones; each address family is ordered separately
    %%> - plans one allow per selected protocol and applicable address family; a port range stays a range
    %%> - numbered positions account for earlier planned changes
    %%> Why native:
    %%> - official cfgmgmt firewall cannot insert at a position or express a range; each mutation is one UFW argv over official SSH, with no copied script
    alt changes needed
      %% path: changes-needed
      SW->>N: Change the rules<br/>one ufw argv · @swamp/ssh exec
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - executes changes sequentially; a native guard skips entries requiring no work
      %%> - moves a misplaced line by deleting and reinserting it; preserves unrelated rules
    else already applied
      %% path: already-applied
      Note over SW: Skipped — already applied<br/>firewall.rule_exists
      %%> Result:
      %%> - no mutation; the final read and check must pass before a successful skip is reported
    end
    SW->>N: Read the result<br/>ufw status · @swamp/ssh exec
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - collects the final rows for the one end-state check
    Note over SW: Check the rules
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - a typed check compares every expected line and its narrow-first position; native assert requires the check to pass
    %%> - the public result comes from the check, not a separate report step
    %%> Failure:
    %%> - a missing or misplaced line fails with `firewall.verify_failed`
    alt rules present and ordered
      %% path: succeeded
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal result
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the Gateway's shared reader marks the rule active and the Operation succeeded
    else read, apply or check fails
      %% path: failed
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code · failed step
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the rule stays visible as failed and the Operation fails
      %%> - a native step stopping before a public check result reports `operation.native_failed`
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - previously added lines remain, and a line deleted for reordering may be absent; no rollback
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - repeat after fixing the cause; reuse the record, keep correct lines and repair missing or misplaced ones without duplicates
    end
  end
